@node CMacs Journal
@chapter CMacs Journal: A Local Activity Journal

@cindex journal
@cindex activity journal
@cindex time tracking
@cindex privacy filters

@code{cmacs-journal} keeps a journal of what had your attention: which
window was focused, which buffer was selected, which page a browser
buffer showed, which agent run finished.  It answers ``where did
Tuesday go'', ``what was I looking at when I wrote this'', and ``reopen
what I had open then''.

Four things about it, stated plainly:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@strong{It is off by default.}  Building cmacs
@option{--with-cmacs-journal} records nothing and listens to nothing.
Recording starts when you turn on @code{cmacs-journal-mode} and stops
when you turn it off.  While it records, the mode line says so.
@item
@strong{It is text only.}  Names and titles: an app id, a window title,
a buffer name, a file name, a major mode, a project, a URL.  Never
screen contents, never keystrokes, never the clipboard, never buffer
text.
@item
@strong{It is local.}  Plain files under
@file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/cmacs/journal}, readable only by you.  Nothing is
sent anywhere.  Agents cannot query it unless you say so.
@item
@strong{It is filtered before it is kept.}  Privacy rules see every
event before it is stored anywhere, including in memory.  What a rule
excludes was never recorded, and nothing notes that a rule fired.
@end itemize

@lisp
(cmacs-journal-mode 1)                  ; start recording
(setq cmacs-journal-retention-days 90)  ; keep three months
@end lisp

Built with @option{--with-cmacs-journal} (part of the default flag
set).  @code{IS-CMACS-JOURNAL} is non-nil in a build that has it.  It is
pure Elisp over hooks other subsystems provide; each of those is
optional.

@menu
* Journal Events::       What is recorded, and what is not.
* Journal Storage::      Where it is kept.
* Journal Commands::     The mode, pausing, forgetting.
* Journal Filters::      The privacy rules, in Elisp and in YAML.
* Journal LQL::          Querying it, and what agents see.
* Journal Uses::         Reports, recall, Org clock.
* Journal Options::      Every option.
* Journal Internals::    The pipeline and the YAML primitive.
@end menu

@node Journal Events
@section What Is Recorded

The journal records @dfn{spans}, not samples.  Nothing polls.  A span
opens when something takes the focus and closes when something else
does, so ``Firefox, this title, from 10:02:11 for 340 seconds'' is one
line.

@multitable @columnfractions .2 .8
@headitem Field @tab Meaning
@item @code{time} @tab When the span started (ISO 8601 with offset)
@item @code{end} @tab When it ended
@item @code{duration} @tab Seconds
@item @code{kind} @tab @code{focus}, @code{buffer}, @code{url}, @code{agent} or @code{idle}
@item @code{app} @tab The window's app id; @code{emacs} for a buffer; @code{gsurf} for a page
@item @code{title} @tab The window or page title
@item @code{pid} @tab The window's process id
@item @code{tags} @tab The gowl tags the window is on
@item @code{monitor} @tab The focused monitor's name
@item @code{buffer} @tab The buffer name
@item @code{file} @tab The file the buffer visits, with @file{~} for the home directory
@item @code{mode} @tab The major mode
@item @code{project} @tab The project (@code{project-current}), or the PARA path inside a notes tree
@item @code{url} @tab The URL of a gsurf buffer
@item @code{detail} @tab @code{para:BUCKET} for a notes file, @code{container:NAME} for a gsurf container, the final state of an agent run
@end multitable

Where each kind comes from:

@table @code
@item focus
Under gowl, the focused window, from
@code{cmacs-gowl-focus-changed-functions},
@code{cmacs-gowl-client-title-changed-functions} and
@code{cmacs-gowl-tag-changed-functions}.
@item buffer
The selected window's buffer, from
@code{window-selection-change-functions} and
@code{window-buffer-change-functions}.  Under gowl, Emacs's own frames
are recorded this way and not as @code{focus}, and buffer changes are
ignored while another program's window has the focus.
@item url
A gsurf buffer, from @code{cmacs-gsurf-uri-changed-functions} and
@code{cmacs-gsurf-title-changed-functions}.
@item agent
A brigade run finishing, from
@code{cmacs-brigade-run-finished-functions}.  A point event: the agent,
the task title and the final state.  The run's output is not recorded.
@item idle
No input for @code{cmacs-journal-idle-threshold} seconds while Emacs
itself has the focus.  Emacs cannot see input going to another
program's window, so it does not guess there.
@end table

What is @emph{not} recorded: anything while the session is locked (a
lock closes the open span, and the locked stretch is a gap); anything
while the journal is paused; anything a filter excludes; spans shorter
than @code{cmacs-journal-min-span} (2 seconds), since passing through a
window is not attention.

Everything works without gowl.  A plain pgtk or tty Emacs has no
@code{focus} events and records buffers, URLs and agent runs.

@node Journal Storage
@section Where It Is Kept

One JSON Lines file per day, @file{YYYY-MM-DD.jsonl}, in
@code{cmacs-journal-directory} (default
@file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/cmacs/journal}, that is
@file{~/.local/state/cmacs/journal}).  The directory is mode 0700 and
each file 0600, set explicitly every time rather than left to the
umask.  A line looks like this:

@example
@{"time":"2026-11-20T10:02:11-0500","end":"2026-11-20T10:07:51-0500",
 "duration":340.0,"kind":"focus","app":"firefox",
 "title":"Wayland - Wikipedia","pid":4242,"tags":[2],"monitor":"DP-1"@}
@end example

@noindent
(one line in the file).  JSON Lines because the files are meant to be
read, grepped and deleted by the person they are about.  Closed spans
wait in memory and are written when Emacs has been idle for
@code{cmacs-journal-flush-idle} seconds, at least every
@code{cmacs-journal-flush-interval} seconds, and when Emacs exits.
@code{cmacs-journal-flush} writes them now.
@code{cmacs-journal-retention-days} deletes older day files (@code{nil},
the default, keeps everything).

The file @file{secret} in the same directory is the per-install key
that @code{hash} filters use.  It is 0600 and is never written into the
journal.

@node Journal Commands
@section Commands

@table @code
@item cmacs-journal-mode
Global minor mode: record.  Off by default.
@item cmacs-journal-pause
Stop recording; the open span closes.
@item cmacs-journal-resume
Start again.
@item cmacs-journal-flush
Write queued spans now.
@item cmacs-journal-forget
Delete a time range, or everything matching a regexp.
@item cmacs-journal-filters-reload
Re-read the filter rules.
@item cmacs-journal-filter-test
Show what the filters would make of the current (or a typed) event.
@item cmacs-journal-report
Time by project, app, day or kind, as an Org table.
@item cmacs-journal-at
What was focused and open around a moment.
@item cmacs-journal-reopen
Reopen that moment's files and pages.
@item cmacs-journal-org-clock
Insert CLOCK lines for a project and a day under the heading at point.
@end table

The mode line shows @code{cmacs-journal-mode-line-recording} while
recording and @code{cmacs-journal-mode-line-paused} while the mode is on
but not recording (paused, locked, or stopped because the filters did
not load).

@code{cmacs-journal-forget} deletes from the stored files, from the
spans not yet written, and the span still open.  Give it a time range,
a regexp, or both: a range alone forgets a stretch of time, a regexp
alone forgets everything that mentions something.  A span partly inside
the range goes whole.  Each day file is rewritten through a temporary
file and a rename, so an interruption leaves the old file or the new
one, never half of one; a day with nothing left is deleted.
Interactively it counts what would go and asks before deleting.

@node Journal Filters
@section Filters

@cindex journal filters
@cindex journal-filters.yaml

This is the part that makes a journal like this safe to keep.  Every
event passes through the filters @emph{before} it is kept anywhere:
before the open span, before the in-memory queue, and so before the
files, the LQL source and any agent.

A rule is a @dfn{match} plus @dfn{actions}.

@table @asis
@item match
Field to regexp pairs over any event field (@code{kind}, @code{app},
@code{title}, @code{pid}, @code{tags}, @code{monitor}, @code{buffer},
@code{file}, @code{mode}, @code{project}, @code{url}, @code{detail}).
All of them must match.  A field the event does not have never matches.
In Elisp the match may instead be a predicate function called with the
event.
@item @code{exclude}
Drop the whole event.  The open span closes and the time becomes an
untracked gap, exactly as if nothing had been focused.  There is no
placeholder and no counter.
@item @code{drop}
A list of fields to blank.
@item @code{replace}
A list of field / regexp / replacement: sanitise @emph{part} of a field,
such as a query string or an account number.  @samp{\N} in the
replacement refers to a group.
@item @code{hash}
A list of fields replaced by a short keyed hash (@samp{#3fa9c1e07b2d}).
The same text always gives the same hash on this machine, so durations
still add up per document or per site without the journal holding the
text.  The key is the per-install secret; another machine gives
different hashes.
@end table

Three properties are kept on purpose:

@enumerate
@item
Every rule is matched against the event @emph{as it arrived}; the
actions are applied afterwards.  One rule's @code{drop} can never hide
the text another rule's @code{exclude} was waiting for, whatever order
they are in.
@item
Nothing records which rule matched.  Not in the event, not in a log,
not in a message.  A note that ``rule 4 fired at 14:02'' is the thing
the rule was written to keep out.
@item
The filters @emph{fail closed} (see below).
@end enumerate

Matching ignores case by default
(@code{cmacs-journal-filter-case-fold}): a rule that matches too much
loses a title, and a rule that matches too little records one.

The same rule set is also what every model-facing surface is filtered
by, whether or not the journal is on; @pxref{CMacs Redact}.  For that
there is one more field, @code{text}: the journal never has it (it
records no text), so a rule on @code{text} changes nothing here.  It is
the field of free text shown to a model---a clipboard entry, text read
off the screen.

@subsection The two layers, and why there are two

The rules come from three places and are merged into one set.

@subsubheading Elisp: @code{cmacs-journal-filters}

@lisp
(setq cmacs-journal-filters
      '((:match ((:app . "^org\\.keepassxc")) :exclude t)
        (:match ((:url . "bank")) :drop (:url :title))
        (:match ((:title . "Invoice"))
         :replace ((:title "[0-9]\\@{6,\\@}" "***"))
         :hash (:file))
        ;; a predicate instead of regexps
        (:match (lambda (event)
                  (equal (plist-get event :monitor) "HDMI-A-1"))
         :exclude t)))
@end lisp

These are Emacs regexps.  This layer is for rules you do not mind anyone
reading, because your Emacs configuration is very often a tracked,
shared or published repository.

@subsubheading YAML: @code{cmacs-journal-filter-files}

The default is one file, @file{~/.config/cmacs/journal-filters.yaml}.

@example
filters:
  - match: @{app: '^org\.keepassxc'@}
    exclude: true
  - match: @{url: 'bank'@}
    drop: [url, title]
  - match: @{title: 'Invoice'@}
    replace: [@{field: title, regexp: '\d@{6,@}', with: '***'@}]
    hash: [file]
@end example

@strong{The YAML layer exists because the exclusion rules themselves
are sensitive.}  A rule that says ``never record windows whose title
contains the name of this clinic, this lawyer, this employer I am
interviewing with'' tells a reader exactly what the rule was written to
hide.  Rules like that must not be in a dotfiles repository, and a file
with its own path outside the tracked tree is the simplest way to make
that true.  Keep it out of Stow, out of git, and readable only by you.
It expresses exactly the same rule set as the Elisp variable; nothing
can be said in one that cannot be said in the other, except the
predicate function.

Regexps in a YAML file are written the way they are everywhere else ---
@samp{\d}, @samp{\s}, @samp{\w}, @samp{(a|b)}, @samp{(?:...)},
@samp{@{6,@}}, a leading @samp{(?i)} --- and translated to Emacs
regexps.  A construct with no Emacs equivalent (lookahead, lookbehind,
named groups, @samp{\p@{...@}}) is an error, which stops the recording
rather than matching something else.

The files are re-read when they change: their directories are watched
with file notification, and the flush timer compares modification times
as a fallback.  @code{cmacs-journal-filters-reload} does it by hand.
When the rules change, the open span closes and whatever has the focus
is looked at again under the new rules.

@subsubheading Shipped defaults: @code{cmacs-journal-default-filters}

A third variable, in the same Elisp shape, holds the rules cmacs ships.
They exclude password managers and keyrings (KeePassXC, Bitwarden,
1Password, GNOME Secrets, Seahorse, KWallet and others), authentication
and passphrase prompts (polkit, pinentry, askpass, ``Authentication
Required''), private and incognito browser windows (by the title
browsers give them), Tor Browser, gsurf's temporary containers, and
Emacs buffers visiting @file{.gpg} files, @file{.authinfo}, the password
store, @file{~/.ssh} and @file{.bashrc-secrets}.  Set it to @code{nil}
to drop them.

@subsection Failing closed

If a listed YAML file @emph{exists} but cannot be read, cannot be
parsed, or holds a top-level key, a rule key, an action, a field name or
a regexp the journal does not understand, there is no usable rule set
and the journal records @emph{nothing}: @code{cmacs-journal-mode} stays
on but stopped, the mode line shows the paused indicator, and a warning
names the file and the problem (never the text of a regexp).  The same
goes for an invalid rule in the Elisp variables.  Fix the file and the
recording starts again on its own; a pause you asked for with
@code{cmacs-journal-pause} is never overridden.

A file that does not exist is fine and means no rules from it.

The reasoning: a typo in @code{exclude} must not silently become
``record everything''.  The one direction in which this tool is allowed
to be wrong is recording too little.

YAML is read by a C primitive, @code{cmacs-yaml-parse-file}
(@pxref{Journal Internals}).  A cmacs built without it treats a filter
file that is present as a failure, not as an empty rule set.

@subsection Trying rules out

@kbd{M-x cmacs-journal-filter-test} shows the event the journal would
record right now and what the filters would make of it.  With a prefix
argument it asks for the fields of a sample instead, so you can check a
rule against a title you would rather not have on screen.  It shows the
result, never which rule produced it.

@node Journal LQL
@section Querying It: the LQL Source

When cmacs has LQL (@option{--with-cmacs-lql}) and the journal is
loaded, the source @code{activity} is defined.  It reads the last
@code{cmacs-journal-lql-days} days (default 14) of stored spans plus
those not yet written.

@multitable @columnfractions .6 .4
@headitem Column @tab Type
@item @code{time}, @code{end} @tab timestamp
@item @code{duration} @tab duration (seconds)
@item @code{kind}, @code{app}, @code{title}, @code{buffer}, @code{file}, @code{mode}, @code{project}, @code{url}, @code{monitor}, @code{detail}, @code{context} @tab string
@item @code{tags} @tab array
@end multitable

The snapshot is cached on a generation counter that moves when a span
closes, is written, or is forgotten, so repeated queries do not re-read
the files.

@subsection Agents

By default @strong{agents get no rows}.
@code{cmacs-journal-expose-to-agents} (default @code{nil}) decides
whether the @code{activity} source and @code{cmacs-journal-events}
return anything to a caller that is not you.

The journal tells the two apart with two signals, either of which is
enough:

@itemize @bullet
@item
A brigade tool call.  Every tool call, from an in-process agent, a CLI
agent over the MCP relay or an external MCP client, goes through one
function, @code{cmacs-brigade-call-tool}.  The journal puts an around
advice on it that binds a flag for the dynamic extent of the call.  A
dynamic binding cannot be left set by a tool that signals, and it covers
exactly the call.
@item
cmacs's RPC dispatch.  Everything arriving over MCP, D-Bus,
@command{emacsctl} and bacon is evaluated with
@code{inhibit-interaction} bound.  This also catches your own
@command{emacsctl lql} from a terminal: over that channel you are
indistinguishable from a program, and the journal assumes the program.
@end itemize

An LQL source's rows are snapshotted on the editor thread when the query
is submitted, inside whichever of those extents applies, so that is when
the decision is made.  Who is asking is part of the snapshot's cache
key, so a snapshot made for you is never the one handed to an agent.

What this is @emph{not}: a sandbox.  An agent you have allowed to
evaluate Lisp or to read files can read the day files directly.  The
switch keeps the journal out of the tools an agent reaches for by
default; the filters are what keep sensitive text out of the journal in
the first place.

@node Journal Uses
@section Using It

@subsection Reports

@kbd{M-x cmacs-journal-report} asks for a span (@code{today},
@code{yesterday}, @code{week}, @code{month}, a number of days, or a
@samp{YYYY-MM-DD} date) and a grouping (@code{project}, @code{app},
@code{day}, @code{kind}) and shows an Org table of time and share.  Only
the kinds in @code{cmacs-journal-report-kinds} (focus, buffer, url)
count; idle time and agent runs are left out.  Time outside any project
is listed under the app it went to.

@vindex cmacs-journal-context-lookback-days
The grouping @code{context} is also offered.  Time then goes to the
named context (@pxref{CMacs Context}) that was active: the one whose
switch event (kind @code{context}, a point with no duration, never
counted as time) most recently precedes the span.  A span that crosses
a switch is split at the switch.  Time before the first switch known is
under @samp{(none)}; the context already active when the range begins
is looked for in the @code{cmacs-journal-context-lookback-days} (7) days
before it and no further.  @code{cmacs-journal-at} names the context of
its moment, and the LQL @code{activity} source has a @code{context}
column: the context a row started in (rows are not split; null for
none).

@subsection Org dynamic block

@example
#+BEGIN: cmacs-journal :span today :by project
#+END:
@end example

@kbd{C-c C-c} on the block writes the same table.  @code{:span} and
@code{:by} take the values the report command does.

@subsection Recall

@kbd{M-x cmacs-journal-at} reads a date and time the Org way and lists
what the journal has within @code{cmacs-journal-at-window} seconds of
it, marking the span that covers the moment.

@kbd{M-x cmacs-journal-reopen} visits that moment's files that still
exist and hands its URLs to @code{browse-url}.  Windows of other
programs are listed, not started again: the journal knows a title, not
a command line.  Fields a filter hashed or blanked are skipped.

@kbd{M-x cmacs-journal-restore} goes further: with
@code{cmacs-journal-checkpoint-mode} on, the journal also records what
was open and how it was arranged, and a moment (``an hour ago'',
``Tuesday 3pm'') can be put back---files, the Emacs window layout,
pages, and applications on their tags---after you confirm a plan.
@kbd{R} on a line of @code{cmacs-journal-at} output, or @kbd{C-c C-r} on
a row of a report by day, shows that plan.  @xref{CMacs Journal
Restore}.

@subsection Org clock

@kbd{M-x cmacs-journal-org-clock} asks for a day and one of the projects
the journal has for it, and inserts @samp{CLOCK:} lines under the
heading at point, joining spans closer than
@code{cmacs-journal-clock-merge-gap} seconds.  This is the only way the
journal reaches an Org file and it happens only when you run it.
Nothing is ever written to your Org files automatically.

