@node Journal Internals
@section Internals

@example
hook --> raw event --> FILTERS --> open span --> queue --> day files
                                                      \--> LQL source
@end example

Two rules shape the pipeline.

@strong{Hook functions are O(1), never signal and never do I/O.}  They
run on gowl's hooks, where under @command{emacs --gowl} Emacs is the
desktop; a hook that signals or waits there is a frozen session, not a
missing journal line.  So a hook builds a small plist, or just notes the
time and arms a zero-delay timer, and returns inside a
@code{condition-case}.  A client that vanished between the signal and
the hook is recorded as ``nothing focused''.  Anything that touches the
disk happens in a timer: finding a buffer's project
(@code{project-current} stats the filesystem; the answer is cached per
buffer and directory, and a remote directory is never asked, so the
journal cannot open a Tramp connection), and writing the day file.

@strong{Filters run before the open span.}
@code{cmacs-journal--observe} hands the raw event to
@code{cmacs-journal-filter-apply} and keeps only what comes back.
Coalescing compares filtered events, so hashed or blanked titles extend
a span like any others.

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@headitem File @tab What
@item @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-journal.el} @tab Spans, hooks, storage, forget, the mode, the LQL source
@item @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-journal-filter.el} @tab Rules: compiling, YAML, the regexp translation, applying
@item @file{lisp/cmacs/cmacs-journal-report.el} @tab Reports, the dynamic block, recall, Org clock
@item @file{cmacs/glib/cmacs-yaml.c} @tab @code{cmacs-yaml-parse-file}
@item @file{test/cmacs/cmacs-journal-tests.el} @tab ERT; needs no compositor
@end multitable

@defun cmacs-yaml-parse-file file
Return the first document of the YAML file @var{file} as JSON text,
which @code{json-parse-string} turns into data; going through JSON text
leaves the choice of alist, plist or hash table to the caller.  Signals
an @code{error} carrying yaml-glib's message when @var{file} cannot be
read or is not valid YAML.  A general primitive, not specific to the
journal.
@end defun

Emacs has no YAML reader, and cmacs already links yaml-glib.  yaml-glib
is not on the @option{--with-cmacs-glib} link line by itself: its
objects reach the binary inside @file{libbacon-1.0.a}.  So the function
is compiled only when @code{HAVE_CMACS_YAML} is defined (a bundled
bacon), and Lisp callers test
@code{(fboundp 'cmacs-yaml-parse-file)}.
